{
  "image_name": "1b976c6e371abcbdb2c498f9a2de6442.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how primary cilium resorption is linked to the cell cycle and to brain development. At the top, a circular sequence shows a cell moving from G0/G1, where a primary cilium is present and attached to the basal body, through S and G2 as the cilium is resorbed, into mitosis and then cytokinesis, after which daughter cells can rebuild cilia. The overall message is that removing the cilium is coordinated with cell-cycle progression.\n\nThe middle section illustrates several visible ways cilia can disappear. These include apparent internalization back into the cell, progressive disassembly, loss of the ciliary tip (“decapitation”), and shedding of larger ciliary material. Together these mechanisms suggest that ciliary resorption is not a single route but a set of related processes.\n\nThe bottom section connects ciliary resorption to size control of the cerebral cortex. In apical radial glial cells, cilium dynamics appear to influence whether cells expand the neural stem cell pool by symmetric division or generate neurons through asymmetric division and intermediate progenitors. The diagram links this to later neuronal migration toward the cortical plate and formation of dendrites and axons, implying that altered ciliary resorption can contribute to microcephaly.",
